<h2>The marriage and the suit</h2>
<p>The parties married on 15 July 2024 at Chandigarh after a roka ceremony that April. Within weeks, the marriage collapsed. The husband's case was stark: the woman he married as Komal Gupta, a Hindu, was in fact Yasmeen, daughter of Mohd. Shahid, and official records bore that identity. He sued for a declaration of nullity. The appellant's answer was that she is a Hindu, daughter of Sh. Manoj Kumar Gupta, and that the "Yasmeen" entries traced to an error by her mother at school admission, carried forward into later records.</p>
<p>After the Supreme Court transferred the suit to the Family Court at Karkardooma (Transfer Petition (C) No. 1471/2025, order of 4 December 2025), the husband applied under Order XII Rule 6 read with Section 151 CPC for a decree on admissions, relying on the Aadhaar card, CBSE Class X and XII certificates, an ICICI Bank statement and an employment letter — all in the name Yasmeen. On 16 September 2026 the Family Court allowed the application and declared the marriage void ab initio. The wife appealed.</p>

<h2>How the Division Bench reasoned</h2>
<div class="tiles">
  <div class="tile"><strong>Independent, consistent records.</strong> The documents were issued or maintained by independent bodies — UIDAI, CBSE, a bank, an employer — and their genuineness was not disputed. The appellant's own pleadings acknowledged they bore the Yasmeen particulars, including salary credits around July 2024.</div>
  <div class="tile"><strong>No proof of the claimed parentage.</strong> No birth certificate or official record proved that Manoj Kumar Gupta is the appellant's father. A Shaadi.com profile is not proof of its contents; a bank certificate marking him "U/G" (guardian) does not establish paternity; and a paternity report obtained privately, without court direction or supervision, is not conclusive.</div>
  <div class="tile"><strong>Admissions can be constructive.</strong> Under Order XII Rule 6, an admission need not be express; the court may act on the pleadings and documents read as a whole.</div>
  <div class="tile"><strong>Statute over equity.</strong> Under Section 2 HMA the Act applies to Hindus, and following Gullipilli Sowria Raj, the Section 5 conditions are mandatory. Waiver, acquiescence and estoppel cannot validate a marriage that breaches them.</div>
</div>
<p>The appeal was dismissed and the Family Court's decree upheld. The Bench noted that the appellant's separate civil suit seeking correction of her records (filed in April 2026) remains pending — it did not treat the claimed correction as established.</p>

<h2>The procedural lesson: nullity without trial</h2>
<p>Matrimonial litigation usually grinds through issues, evidence and cross-examination. This case is a reminder that Order XII Rule 6 is available in Family Court proceedings too, and that a respondent who concedes the authenticity of the decisive documents while disputing only their explanation may find the suit decided on admissions. The explanation — here, a school-admission error replicated across Aadhaar, board certificates, banking and employment records — must itself be proved, and until it is, the records speak.</p>
